# No unilateral cost optimizations

Never make a cost-saving decision that reduces quality, capability, or fidelity
without asking the operator first. This includes (but is not limited to):

- Truncating embedding dimensions, quantizing vectors, or shrinking indexes
- Picking a smaller/cheaper model, lower reasoning effort, or fewer candidates
- Reducing storage, retention, sampling rates, or corpus coverage
- Downsizing compute (VM sizes, GPU counts, sandbox counts, concurrency)

Default to the highest-quality option. If cost is a genuine concern, present the
trade-off and let the operator decide - do not decide for them. Saving money is not an
achievement here; capability is.
